it, perhaps even the majority, hold the opposed opinion. I'm absolutely gobsmacked that you found any use for the thing; I personally sold off my copy at a slight loss just to free up the shelf space.<br /> <br /> At its peak in the late 90s, White Wolf had multiple dozens of employees, half a dozen concurrently-printed game lines, and was a legitimate contender for being the most popular and prolific publisher in the tabletop market. Then nWoD happened and mysteriously, two years later the company was bought by Vikings for a song. But this isn't financial failure or anything like that; it's totally the market's fault. Nevermind that new companies such as Paizo have emerged and gone gangbusters even in the Great Recession (Pathfinder launched in 2009).<br /> <br /> And nWoD being a departure from the &quot;supplement treadmill...&quot; You'll have to clarify, because <a href=\"http://whitewolf.wikia.com/wiki/Vampire:_The_Requiem_books\" target=\"_blank\">it doesn't look that way to me</a>.<br /> <br /> <br /> OK, your subjective play experience contradicts the fact that there is no longer a company called &quot;White Wolf&quot; in existence.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wow. You really are an angry little man, aren't you? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Look, I understand that you're all worked up because WW of the now doesn't look like WW of the 90s, and you've nowhere you can wear your trenchcoat and mirrorshades anymore. But that's no reason to get all shrieky about it. I see a brand that has developed over the years, through good and bad, but continues to produce great games. You don't. That's fine. Perhaps you need to take a break from whatever it is that's making you so irritable, though. It can't be good for your health.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"l